---
/**
 * The panel, declared before anyone sees a score.
 *
 * A number from an unnamed "AI" is an appeal to authority with no authority
 * behind it. Naming the three labs, saying what each model actually is, and
 * admitting where a lab publishes nothing is the difference between a score a
 * reader can weigh and a score they have to take on faith.
 *
 * The specs are the honest part and the constraint that shaped the panel. Two
 * of these models publish their architecture, so their entries carry real
 * numbers; the others disclose nothing, and those entries say "undisclosed"
 * rather than carrying a parameter count invented to match the others. A single
 * fabricated figure on a page whose whole premise is transparency would
 * discredit everything around it.
 *
 * A juror is rendered EXACTLY as it is on a company page: character, title and
 * lens first, then the model underneath. Two shapes for one panel is how a
 * reader ends up thinking the landing page and the profile describe different
 * things — and both halves are load-bearing. The character is who wrote the
 * words; the model is what makes the claim checkable.
 *
 * Everything here is derived from PANELISTS and WRITER. Nothing about the panel
 * is spelled out in prose on this page, because prose does not update itself
 * when a seat changes and the last roster change left three pages disagreeing.
 *
 * Styles: .aol-panel__* in src/styles/components.css
 */
import PixelIcon from "./PixelIcon.astro";
import { PANELISTS, WRITER, QUESTIONS, panelLabs } from "../../lib/rankings";

interface Props {
  /** Show the three questions alongside the panel. */
  questions?: boolean;
}
const { questions = true } = Astro.props;
---

<div class="aol-panel">
  <h2 class="aol-rma__h2">The panelists.</h2>
  {/* The lab list is computed, never typed. It was typed once and went stale
      the day a seat changed, leaving the landing page naming a lab that was no
      longer on the panel. */}
  <p class="aol-rma__note">
    Three models from three different labs — {panelLabs()} — each handed the
    same pages and asked the same three questions. Each holds one fixed lens
    across all three, so the engineer answers the outlook question as an
    engineer. They cannot see each other's answers — the calls run at the same
    moment, which is what makes agreement between them worth something.
  </p>

  <ul class="aol-panel__list">
    {PANELISTS.map((p) => (
      <li class="aol-panel__member">
        <span class="aol-panel__lab">{p.lab}</span>
        <span class="aol-panel__name">{p.character}</span>
        <p class="aol-panel__role">{p.title} · {p.lens}</p>
        <p class="aol-panel__bio">{p.bio}</p>
        <p class="aol-panel__spec">{p.name} — {p.spec}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <p class="aol-panel__writer">
    <PixelIcon name="quote-left" size={12} />
    <span>
      {/* The explicit {" "} before the spec is load-bearing: Astro trims the
          whitespace around an expression that sits alone on its own line, so
          leaving it to the template produced "(OpenAI).Parameter count". The
          {", "} after the name is belt-and-braces rather than a fix — that one
          was fine, and only looked broken through a tag-stripping text dump. */}
      <strong>{WRITER.character}</strong>{", "}{WRITER.title}, writes the summary
      and scores nothing. {WRITER.mandate} Played by {WRITER.name} ({WRITER.lab}).
      {" "}{WRITER.spec}
    </span>
  </p>

  {questions ? (
    <>
      <h2 class="aol-rma__h2">The three questions.</h2>
      <ul class="aol-rma__axis-list">
        {QUESTIONS.map((q) => (
          <li class="aol-rma__axis">
            <span class="aol-rma__axis-icon"><PixelIcon name={q.icon as any} size={24} /></span>
            <span class="aol-rma__axis-body">
              <span class="aol-rma__axis-name">{q.label}<em>hard no → extremely</em></span>
              {/* The question itself, not a persona. Personas used to hang off
                  the questions — one hat per question, worn by all three
                  jurors. They now belong to the jurors, who hold one lens
                  across all three questions, so there is nothing per-question
                  left to print here. */}
              <span class="aol-rma__axis-q">{q.question}</span>
            </span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="aol-rma__note">
        Every panelist answers all three, so a company carries nine answers.
        Each is one of five words — <em>hard no, no, kinda, yes, extremely</em> —
        and nobody picks a number. The words become the score, the score becomes
        the average, and the total is out of thirty.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : null}
</div>
